---
module: requires_chain
func_id: share-exported-concepts
---

***conformance tests***

# exported-concept-is-visible
`exported_concepts` exports and defines `- ⟨Concept⟩ is a concept.`; a module that requires it and defines `- ⟨DependantConcept⟩ depends on the ⟨Concept⟩ concept.` parses.

# export-of-imported-concept
`exported_concepts_nested` imports `exported_concepts_base` (which defines Concept) and exports `- ⟨NestedConcept⟩ depends on the ⟨Concept⟩ concept.`; a module requiring it and using NestedConcept parses.

# export-arrives-in-definitions
A module that requires `exported_concepts` and has no `definitions` section gets the definitions [{markdown `- ⟨Concept⟩ is a concept.`}].

# middle-module-sees-base-exports
`shared_export_base` exports KeyStore; `shared_export_middle` requires [base] and uses KeyStore; `shared_export_top` requires [base, middle] and uses KeyStore; parsing `shared_export_top` succeeds.

# base-module-may-export-nothing
Base exports nothing, Middle requires [Base] and exports MiddleConcept, Top requires [Base, Middle] and uses MiddleConcept; the chain of Top is Base, Middle, Top.

***acceptance tests***

# exports-are-not-transitive
`exported_concepts_transitive_l2` exports Concept, `exported_concepts_transitive_l1` requires it, and a module requiring only l1 and mentioning Concept raises `PlainSyntaxError` holding `not defined`.

# concept-not-exported
A module requiring `exported_concepts_not_declared`, which defines Concept without exporting it, and mentioning Concept raises `PlainSyntaxError` holding `not defined`.

# redefined-export
Base exports and defines BaseConcept, Middle requires [Base] and defines BaseConcept again, Top requires [Base, Middle]; listing the chain of Top raises `PlainSyntaxError` holding `Concepts were defined multiple times: ⟨BaseConcept⟩`.
